# R2 results: one MCP `tools/call`, three schemas

**Pre-registration:** `PREREGISTRATION.md` sha256 `eddf681633f728b700e1d3b7bc6662ac9ddde1702bee8297ff4a8d3fa8410183`,
frozen 2026-11-09T22:58:56Z, frozen by agent before run; not owner-reviewed.

**Rig stamp (§7b):** Beelink, AMD Ryzen 7 5800H (14 threads visible to WSL2, 47 GiB), WSL2 kernel
6.6.87.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2, Windows power plan not checked from inside the guest. Run date 2026-11-09.
DuckDB 1.5.3 (default threads = 14), pyarrow 23.0.1, Python 3.12.3, MCP Python SDK 2.3.0. Clock: chrony; `chronyc
tracking` read 0.32 s slow at the start of the formal timing run (above the methodology's milliseconds target,
probably still slewing) and 0.00003 s at the end. Every duration is from `time.perf_counter` (monotonic), so the
offset does not enter the latencies, but I note it rather than hide it. **Isolation was violated**: other lab
agents were running benches on the same host during the formal run (1-minute load average 25 at one check
afterwards), so absolute times are inflated and noisy; the within-pass ratios are the claim. See Deviations.

**Tier:** B for method (first-party, single machine, synthetic corpus for the timing part, real wire capture for the
mapping part). Schema facts come from the pinned upstream files in `ref/` (vendor/standards-body documentation of
what a field is, Tier B for what a feature does).

## Question

One real MCP `tools/call` exchange, recorded in OCSF (main, with the `ai_operation` profile, and the #1754 draft
class), OpenTelemetry GenAI + MCP semantic conventions, and OWASP AOS on OCSF: what does each keep typed, keep as an
untyped blob, push to `unmapped`/custom attributes, or drop; and what does the unmapped-JSON shape cost at query time?

## Part 1: capture and the loss table

Ten `tools/call` requests went from the SDK `ClientSession` through a stdio tee (`proxy.py`) to an SDK `MCPServer`
backed by DuckDB (`server.py`); 25 wire messages are in `capture/wire.jsonl` (initialize, initialized, tools/list,
10 request/response pairs). Six calls succeeded and four returned `isError: true` (SQL syntax error, rejected
`DROP`, missing table, unknown tool). Two things about the wire itself, before any schema gets involved:

- The SDK client offered and the server accepted protocol `2025-11-25`, although SDK 2.3.0 advertises
  `LATEST_PROTOCOL_VERSION = 2026-07-28`; the default client negotiates the older version.
- The SDK server masks tool exceptions: the SQL syntax error, the rejected `DROP` and the missing table all reach
  the wire as the same text, `Error executing tool <name>`. The cause never leaves the server process, so no
  client-side record in any schema can carry it. The unknown tool came back as a tool result with `isError`, not a
  JSON-RPC error, so `rpc.response.status_code` / a JSON-RPC error code never appears in this capture.

The fact inventory is 33 facts per call (protocol, tool, payload, outcome, server, client, tool advertisement,
timing, and six harness-supplied agent-context facts, since no agent runtime or model is in the loop). Each schema's
mapping is a declared spec in `map_schemas.py`; the script writes every fact into the record, reads it back from the
emitted record, and checks every OCSF path against the pinned OCSF main object definitions and every OTel key against
the pinned registries. All paths resolved (0 failures). Records are in `results/records_*.jsonl`.

| class | OCSF main (6003 + `ai_operation`) | OCSF #1754 draft | OTel GenAI + MCP | AOS on OCSF (example 2, primary) | AOS example 1 (sensitivity) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| TYPED | 24 | 22 | 19 | 11 | 8 |
| HASHED (fingerprint only) | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| BLOB (`json_t` / `any`) | 3 | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 |
| UNMAPPED (`unmapped` / `sdw.*` custom) | 3 | 5 | 11 | 22 | 25 |
| DROPPED | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |

The per-fact table with every target path is `results/loss_table.md` (machine-readable: `results/loss_table.json`).
What drives the differences:

- **OCSF main** types the most because `ai_capability` (on main, not in the 1.9.0 release) has typed slots for the
  server identity, transport (`transport_id` 1 = stdio), primitive kind, the request id (`transaction_uid`) and all
  four MCP annotations (`is_readonly`, `is_destructive`, `is_idempotent`, `is_open_world`). It carries the tool
  description and input/output schemas only as fingerprints, so the text is gone but a change is detectable. Its
  unmapped residue is the JSON-RPC version, the server instructions and the server capabilities.
- **OCSF #1754 draft** (`ai_agent_activity`, Tool Use) has no `api` object, so the MCP method and protocol version
  fall to `unmapped`; otherwise it inherits the `ai_operation` profile through the Application category and matches
  OCSF main. Its own `ai_agent_tool_use_info` adds a typed tool `name` and `is_pre_occurrence`, with input and
  response as `json_t`.
- **OTel** types the protocol layer most completely (it is the only one with a typed JSON-RPC version, and
  `mcp.method.name`, `jsonrpc.request.id`, `mcp.protocol.version`, `network.transport = pipe`, `error.type =
  tool_error`) and keeps the tool description as a plain string. It has no attribute for the MCP server's name or
  version on a tool span, for the tool annotations, for the input/output schema on an `execute_tool` span, or for an
  agent instance id, so those 11 facts go to custom `sdw.*` attributes. Every attribute used is `stability:
  development`.
- **AOS** puts the agent in `actor.user` (type_id 99, "AI Agent"), the server in `api.service`, and nearly
  everything agent- or tool-specific under `unmapped.aos`. The two examples on the same AOS page disagree:
  example 2 puts the tool name in `api.operation` (typed) and the session in `actor.session.uid`, example 1 puts
  `tools/call` in `api.operation` and the tool name and session in `unmapped`. AOS's own SIEM query examples filter
  on `unmapped.asop.*` (sic, "asop" not "aos"), so a consumer has to know which spelling a producer used.

**OCSF validator check.** All 10 AOS records validate against the public OCSF 1.9.0 validator with zero errors (two
warnings: `activity_name` "Tool Execution" does not match activity_id 1 "Create", and metadata.version 1.0.0). All 10
OCSF-main records fail on `ai_capability` (not in 1.9.0) and on version `1.10.0-dev`, which is expected for a main-
branch record. The point worth keeping is that schema-valid and queryable are different properties: the record with
22 facts in `unmapped` is the one that passes (`results/ocsf_validator_1.9.0.json`).

### Hypotheses H1 to H3

- **H1 supported.** OCSF main types 24 facts against AOS's 11 (8 under example 1). Of the 12 agent- and
  tool-specific facts named in H1 (tool name, four annotations, model name, model provider, session, arguments,
  result content, structured content, isError), AOS puts 9 in `unmapped` under example 2 and 11 under example 1,
  above the "at least half" threshold.
- **H2 supported.** On the six protocol-level facts (method, request id, protocol version, session, tool name,
  error indicator) OTel and OCSF main both type 6 of 6, and OTel has no typed attribute for the annotations or for a
  schema fingerprint. OCSF main's `api.version` for the MCP protocol version is my judgment call; without it OCSF
  main types 5 of 6.
- **H3 supported.** No schema types individual argument values: OCSF main and #1754 carry arguments and results as
  `json_t`, OTel as opt-in `any`, AOS in `unmapped`.

## Part 2: query-time cost of the unmapped shape

`gen_corpus.py` generated 1,000,000 synthetic calls (and a 100,000 sweep point) from one seeded RNG and wrote them
three ways, each with DuckDB `COPY ... COMPRESSION zstd, ROW_GROUP_SIZE 122880`, ordered by time; footers read back
ZSTD and 9 row groups at 1M for all three. `run_queries.py` ran the five pre-registered hunts, 2 warmups + 7 timed
trials per (layout, query), in 3 separate processes with rotated layout order, so 21 samples per cell. Answers were
identical across all three layouts for every query in every pass. Warm cache only (no root page-cache purge).

1M calls, pooled over 21 samples per cell (full table, including 100k, in `results/timing_summary.md`):

| query | L1 typed OCSF median ms (CV) | L2 OTel attributes map | L3 AOS `unmapped` JSON | L3 / L1 | L2 / L1 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Q1 tool by agent | 7.3 (54%) | 235.5 (8%) | 61.3 (21%) | 8.4x | 32.3x |
| Q2 argument hunt (null control) | 36.1 (14%) | 265.3 (18%) | 190.9 (5%) | 5.3x | 7.4x |
| Q3 tool breadth on errors | 24.5 (72%) | 260.2 (10%) | 131.4 (16%) | 5.4x | 10.6x |
| Q4 failure bursts | 21.0 (65%) | 225.2 (9%) | 98.8 (22%) | 4.7x | 10.7x |
| Q5 off-allowlist anti-join | 8.3 (59%) | 270.0 (8%) | 187.1 (7%) | 22.5x | 32.4x |

On disk at 1M: L1 27.4 MB, L2 58.2 MB, L3 30.9 MB. Write time: L1 0.57 s, L2 3.27 s, L3 1.02 s.

The L1 CVs are high because L1 queries finish in single-digit milliseconds, where a scheduler stall from the
concurrent benches is a large fraction of the run; the L1 per-pass medians for Q3 were 7.5, 16.8 and 29.9 ms. The
conservative floor (fastest L3 trial over slowest L1 trial) is still above 1 on every H4 query: Q1 2.2x, Q3 1.19x,
Q4 1.62x, Q5 5.66x. At 100k the L3/L1 ratios were 6.1x, 5.6x, 11.3x, 16.3x and 20.6x for Q1 to Q5, so the direction
does not flip with scale.

### Hypotheses H4, H4b, H5

- **H4 supported** on the pooled medians: L3/L1 is 8.4x, 5.4x, 4.7x and 22.5x on Q1, Q3, Q4, Q5, every one above the
  3x prediction and each delta far outside the pooled CV. The prediction that the OTel map would sit between typed and
  unmapped **failed**: L2 was the slowest layout on every query, 10.6x to 32.4x L1. In DuckDB a lookup on a
  `MAP(VARCHAR, VARCHAR)` reads the whole map column, which in L2 includes the opt-in argument and result payloads,
  so every hunt pays to read the payloads even when it only filters on the tool name.
- **H4b refuted.** The argument hunt was predicted to be about equal because arguments are JSON in every layout, but
  L3 was 5.3x and L2 7.4x L1. L1 filters on a typed tool name first and parses only the small `api.request.data`
  string for the roughly 25% of calls (weight 30 of 121) that are `run_query`; L3 has to parse the whole `unmapped` document for every row just
  to find the tool name, before it ever reaches the arguments. So the cost of `unmapped` shows up even in a query
  whose target is untyped in every schema.
- **H5 refuted.** L3 was 1.13x L1 on disk at 1M (predicted at least 1.3x). ZSTD absorbs most of the repeated JSON
  keys. The cost of the unmapped shape is query time, not storage. L2 was 2.12x, but that figure includes trace_id
  and span_id columns (27 MB of the 58 MB) that L1 and L3 do not carry, so it is not a like-for-like size comparison.

## Rerun under lower load (deviation 2)

Same code, 1M corpus, 3 passes × 7 trials, started once the 1-minute load average reached 3.9. Other agents kept
working, though, and the load climbed to 5.7, 7.1 and 8.1 by the end of each pass, so this run is quieter but still
not isolated. Answers were identical across all layouts again. Pooled medians (CV):

| query | L1 typed | L2 OTel map | L3 AOS unmapped | L3 / L1 | L2 / L1 | answers equal |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Q1 tool by agent | 6.6 (62%) | 201.5 (9%) | 56.1 (19%) | 8.5x (floor 2.24x) | 30.4x | True |
| Q2 argument hunt | 35.0 (21%) | 221.6 (9%) | 173.3 (7%) | 4.9x (floor 2.90x) | 6.3x | True |
| Q3 tool breadth on errors | 8.2 (65%) | 227.4 (16%) | 121.1 (24%) | 14.7x (floor 1.57x) | 27.6x | True |
| Q4 failure bursts | 6.7 (73%) | 223.0 (15%) | 108.2 (24%) | 16.1x (floor 1.37x) | 33.2x | True |
| Q5 off-allowlist anti-join | 7.9 (54%) | 222.3 (7%) | 170.7 (9%) | 21.6x (floor 5.31x) | 28.1x | True |

All three conclusions from the formal run hold here:
- L3 is at least 3x L1 on every H4 query.
- L2 is the slowest layout on every query.
- Q2 is not a null result.

The Q3 and Q4 ratios move a lot between the two runs (5.4x to 14.7x, 4.7x to 16.1x). That movement comes almost
entirely from L1's millisecond-scale medians (24.5 to 8.2 ms on Q3), which are the numbers most exposed to
contention. So I would quote these ratios as "roughly 5x to 16x" and not as a point estimate. The Q1 and Q5 ratios
barely moved (8.4x/8.5x and 22.5x/21.6x). Raw data: `results/timing_rerun_n1000000_pass*.json`,
`results/timing_rerun_summary.json`, `results/rerun_log.txt`.

## Exploratory, not pre-registered

`explore_l2_nopayload.py` rewrote L2 without the opt-in argument/result keys (the OTel default, since both are
`opt_in`) to test whether payloads explain the map cost. It ran while the host load average was about 25, and its
results (Q1 255 ms, Q3 747 ms, Q4 732 ms, Q5 775 ms, CV up to 48%) are slower than the formal L2 numbers, which is
implausible on mechanics alone and most likely contention. I do not draw a conclusion from it; the file
(47.4 MB versus 58.2 MB) and the script stay in the directory, and the question stays open.

## Deviations from the pre-registration

1. **Smoke pass discarded.** One 100k pass was run before the formal run to check answer equality; its timings were
   deleted and the formal run started from scratch with the same code.
2. **Isolation.** Methodology §5 requires nothing heavy running during a timing pass. Sibling lab agents were running
   their own benches throughout (seen afterwards: load average 25, several `rerun_measure.py`, `run_planted.py`,
   `grype` and `astro check` processes). I added load-average logging to `run_queries.py` and reran the 1M passes once
   the 1-minute load fell below 4 (or after a ~9-minute wait), recorded above. The formal-run numbers stay as the
   pre-registered result.
3. **HASHED class added.** The rubric did not anticipate OCSF carrying a fingerprint of a fact instead of the fact,
   so I split it out from TYPED rather than count a hash as keeping the description.
4. **AOS example 1 column added** as a sensitivity, because the AOS page's two examples disagree. The primary column
   follows example 2 as pre-registered; the L3 query layout follows example 1, also as pre-registered (tool name and
   session in `unmapped`).
5. **Loss-table OCSF records** got `src_endpoint` (required by 6003) and integer-millisecond `duration` after the
   1.9.0 validator flagged both; neither changes a fact's class.

## Limits

- stdio only. The review asked for one Streamable-HTTP server as well; HTTP adds `Mcp-Session-Id`, HTTP status,
  and endpoint facts, so this inventory undercounts transport facts, and `mcp.session.id` was never exercised.
- Agent and model identity are harness-supplied; no agent runtime emitted them.
- AOS is scored on its published examples; a producer could put more AOS facts into typed OCSF attributes.
- The OTel lake shape (one attributes map) is the ClickHouse-exporter pattern, one choice among several; a
  column-per-attribute landing would behave much more like L1.
- One engine (DuckDB 1.5.3), warm cache, one host under contention, synthetic corpus. Ratios within a pass are the
  claim; absolute milliseconds are not.

